Welcome back to this session on benchmarking. Some time ago,

3
00:00:09.466 --> 00:00:13.199
I did an extensive research in the healthcare industry.

4
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.366
Looking at a lot of different types

5
00:00:16.366 --> 00:00:18.799
of potential defect categories.

6
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:18.833
Into here. For example, here is ICU error rate, per day, per patient.

7
00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:23.966
For example, ICU error rate, per day, per patient.

8
00:00:23.966 --> 00:00:28.799
Showing about a DPMO of 10,000.

9
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.533
Which converts to 3.83 Sigma.

10
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:37.933
Let's take another one. Adverse drug events

11
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:41.833
of medication errors that resulted in death.

12
00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:47.999
58,200 PPM, or about Three Sigma. Wow.

13
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.433
That's a scary thought. Here's a good one.

14
00:00:51.433 --> 00:00:57.966
Hospital negligence about 10,000 PPM or about 3.83 Sigma.

15
00:00:57.966 --> 00:01:04.966
Wow. As we start to look at all of these different types,

16
00:01:04.966 --> 00:01:11.432
we can put those on to a benchmark chart like we've looked at before.

17
00:01:11.433 --> 00:01:11.466
We’re going to pan. There we go.

18
00:01:11.466 --> 00:01:14.466
Over here, we've got parts per million,

19
00:01:14.466 --> 00:01:17.632
which is the same thing as defects per million opportunities.

20
00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:21.499
A hundred, a thousand,

21
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:28.366
10,000 at that level, and so on, up to 100,000, up to a million.

22
00:01:28.366 --> 00:01:34.566
Way up there at the top of the chart. Here's the corresponding Sigma. One Sigma,

23
00:01:34.566 --> 00:01:39.299
two, three, four, five, and six.

24
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.700
And there is the transfer line, right there.

25
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:47.300
Now, let’s take a look at some of these.

26
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:54.300
Look at this. Surgical complications running about Three Sigma.

27
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.700
Drug injuries, about Two Sigma.

28
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.700
So your odds with a drug injury,

29
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:09.133
is about the same as your odds with Internal Revenue Service tax advice, about Two Sigma.

30
00:02:09.133 --> 00:02:13.799
Let’s look over here. Injuries due to negligence. That's a good one.

31
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.566
Right there. About Four Sigma. Prescription errors.

32
00:02:18.566 --> 00:02:23.166
Now look at this one down here. Anesthesiology. Anesthesia errors,

33
00:02:23.166 --> 00:02:27.832
